<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles, Prince of Viana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1421–1461</span> · Also known as Charles of Viana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles, Prince of Viana (1421–1461), was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blanche-i-of-navarre">Blanche I of Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon">John II of Aragon</a> and the rightful heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. His father's refusal to yield the crown led to a civil war in which the Beaumontese supported Charles and the Agramontese his father. A cultivated humanist who translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Ethics, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1461, and his treatment helped provoke the Catalan civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-viana.jpg" alt="Charles, Prince of Viana" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_de_Viana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
